/*
 * DLC-D -- symbolic model of the REPLICATION protocol (Propose / Vote / Commit).
 *
 * R6.1b step 1. Design: spec/r6-1-node-design.md sec 4; roadmap: spec/dlc-d-roadmap.md sec 2.
 * Companion ProVerif cross-check: models/proverif/dlcd-replication.pv.
 * Right-reason bite (a SEPARATE theory): dlcd-replication-bite.spthy.
 *
 * =========================================================================
 * WHAT THIS MODEL IS FOR -- and what it is NOT for
 * =========================================================================
 *
 * DLC-D's agreement, convergence, linearizability and liveness are already
 * MACHINE-CHECKED IN LEAN over the abstract model (DLCD.agreement,
 * replicas_converge_via_consensus, single_linearization, weakfair_terminates)
 * and transported to the deployed Rust core (DLCD.Transport.rust_*). Tamarin
 * does not re-prove any of that, and could not: it is a Dolev-Yao protocol
 * prover, not a consensus-safety prover.
 *
 * What Tamarin is for here is the question Lean CANNOT answer, and which
 * `crates/dlc-d-node` must answer before it may speak over a network:
 *
 *     the Lean guarantees assume a well-formed, authorized log
 *     (`WellFormedLog`, `Authorized` -- the premises of
 *     `rust_capability_safety`). Over a trusted in-process channel those
 *     premises are free. Over a network they are NOT. What cryptographic
 *     structure must the wire have so that an active attacker cannot make
 *     an honest replica apply a command whose premises fail?
 *
 * So this model is a DESIGN-OBLIGATION GENERATOR for the R6.1b wire format:
 * every lemma below is a requirement the socket implementation must satisfy,
 * and the model fixes the message shapes that discharge them.
 *
 * =========================================================================
 * THE PROTOCOL, AND THE ONE DESIGN DECISION THAT MATTERS
 * =========================================================================
 *
 *   Issue_cap    issuer I signs a capability for a command
 *   Propose      leader L signs (slot, cmd) and forwards the capability
 *   Vote         replica R verifies BOTH signatures, spends its ONE vote for
 *                that slot, and signs (slot, cmd)
 *   Commit       a QUORUM CERTIFICATE -- two distinct votes -- is broadcast
 *   Apply        a replica verifies THE QUORUM CERTIFICATE ITSELF and applies
 *
 * The decision: `Apply` does NOT trust the leader. The commit message carries
 * the two vote signatures and the applying replica checks them; the leader's
 * own signature on the commit is deliberately NOT load-bearing (the rule does
 * not even look at it). The weaker alternative -- trust a leader-signed commit
 * -- would make `slot_agreement` conditional on the leader not equivocating,
 * i.e. it would hold only for a crash-fault leader. Checking the certificate
 * makes agreement survive a BYZANTINE leader, and that is what the lemma
 * proves. This is the property the socket implementation MUST preserve: if
 * R6.1b ever "optimizes" Apply into trusting the commit signature, this model
 * stops proving what its name says.
 *
 * =========================================================================
 * MODELLING CHOICES, STATED HONESTLY
 * =========================================================================
 *
 * 1. FIXED ROSTER of three replicas ('r1','r2','r3'), quorum = 2. Bounding
 *    the participant set is standard practice for quorum/voting models in
 *    Tamarin (the literature's e-voting models are likewise fixed at two
 *    voters), and it is REQUIRED here: quorum intersection is what makes
 *    `slot_agreement` true, and with an unbounded replica set two 2-element
 *    quorums need not intersect. The Lean side carries the general statement
 *    (`quorum_intersect` over `Finset (Fin n)`); Tamarin carries the wire
 *    structure at n=3. Neither subsumes the other -- that split is the point.
 *
 * 2. ONE VOTE PER (replica, slot) is enforced structurally: `Open_slot` mints
 *    a LINEAR `VoteToken` and `Vote` consumes it, with `unique_slot_open`
 *    forbidding a second mint. This is the wire counterpart of the node's
 *    `slot == next_slot()` guard. It is load-bearing for `slot_agreement`,
 *    and that is not an assertion of taste: `dlcd-replication-bite.spthy` is
 *    this same theory with that restriction removed, where Tamarin PROVES
 *    (as an exists-trace) that two honest replicas apply DIFFERENT commands
 *    at the same slot with no key compromise at all.
 *
 * 3. Signature checks use the derivation-clean `verify(sig, msg, pk) = true`
 *    idiom rather than pattern-matching `sign(msg, sk)` against `!Pk(P,
 *    pk(sk))`. The older idiom is what `dlc.spthy` uses and it is why that
 *    file reports "Failed to derive Variable(s): skP, skQ" under Tamarin's
 *    message-derivation wellformedness check -- a warning class that the CI
 *    gate does not currently fail on. This file is written to have ZERO
 *    wellformedness warnings, and its CI step fails if any appear.
 *
 * 4. LIVENESS IS NOT MODELLED, by construction. A Dolev-Yao adversary may
 *    drop every message, so no progress property can hold here. Liveness is
 *    Lean's (`weakfair_terminates` under `WeakFair`) and the failure budget
 *    is the contract; the runtime shadow is the over-budget STALL exercised
 *    in `crates/dlc-d-node/tests/cluster.rs`.
 *
 * 5. What the adversary CAN do: read, drop, reorder, replay and inject any
 *    message; register keys; and reveal any long-term key (static
 *    compromise). Every all-traces lemma below carries an explicit reveal
 *    escape clause, so the lemmas say exactly "unless a key leaked".
 */

theory DLCD_Replication
begin

builtins: signing

// =========================================================================
// Restrictions
// =========================================================================

// Signature verification, the derivable way: the rule emits the check as an
// action and this restriction discharges it. See modelling choice 3.
restriction Equality:
  "All x y #i. Eq(x, y) @ #i ==> x = y"

restriction Inequality:
  "All x y #i. Neq(x, y) @ #i ==> not(x = y)"

// One keypair per principal. Without this a principal could hold two keys and
// "its" signature would not be a well-defined notion.
restriction unique_key:
  "All A #i #j. KeyRegistered(A) @ #i & KeyRegistered(A) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

// THE structural heart of agreement: a replica gets exactly ONE vote per slot.
// Removing this is the bite (see dlcd-replication-bite.spthy).
restriction unique_slot_open:
  "All R s #i #j. SlotOpened(R, s) @ #i & SlotOpened(R, s) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

// The fixed roster. Only these three can hold a vote token, so only these
// three can vote -- quorum intersection at n=3 is what `slot_agreement`
// spends. See modelling choice 1.
restriction roster:
  "All R s #i. SlotOpened(R, s) @ #i
     ==> (R = 'r1') | (R = 'r2') | (R = 'r3')"

// =========================================================================
// Keys and compromise
// =========================================================================

rule Register_key:
    [ Fr(~sk) ]
  --[ HonestKey($A, ~sk), KeyRegistered($A) ]->
    [ !Ltk($A, ~sk), !Pk($A, pk(~sk)), Out(pk(~sk)) ]

// Static long-term-key reveal -- the adversary learns a principal's secret.
// Every all-traces lemma below is conditioned on this not having happened.
rule Reveal_ltk:
    [ !Ltk($A, sk) ]
  --[ Reveal($A) ]->
    [ Out(sk) ]

// A replica's single vote for one slot. Minted at most once per (R, slot) by
// `unique_slot_open`, and only for roster members by `roster`.
rule Open_slot:
    [ ]
  --[ SlotOpened($R, $slot) ]->
    [ VoteToken($R, $slot) ]

// =========================================================================
// The protocol
// =========================================================================

// An issuer grants a capability for a command: the wire form of the
// `says`-credential that `commit-I` demands as a subterm and that
// `DLCD.Authorized` quantifies over.
rule Issue_cap:
    [ Fr(~cmd), !Ltk($I, skI) ]
  --[ CapIssued($I, ~cmd) ]->
    [ Out(<'cmd', ~cmd, $I, sign(<'cap', ~cmd>, skI)>) ]

// The leader proposes a capability-carrying command for a slot. It verifies
// the capability before proposing -- not load-bearing for the lemmas (the
// voters check it too, and the leader is not trusted), but it is what the
// implementation does and modelling it keeps the shapes aligned.
rule Propose:
    [ In(<'cmd', cmd, $I, capsig>)
    , !Ltk($L, skL)
    , !Pk($I, pkI)
    ]
  --[ Eq(verify(capsig, <'cap', cmd>, pkI), true)
    , Proposed($L, $slot, cmd)
    ]->
    [ Out(<'propose', $L, $slot, cmd, $I, capsig,
           sign(<'propose', $slot, cmd>, skL)>) ]

// A replica votes. It spends its ONE token for the slot, and it verifies BOTH
// the leader's signature AND the capability. The capability check here is what
// makes `applied_implies_capability` provable: it is the wire-level enforcement
// of the gate that `crates/dlc-d-node` carries but does not check (R6.1a's
// disclosed fence -- `cap` is transported, not verified). This model says
// plainly what the networked node must add.
rule Vote:
    [ VoteToken($R, $slot)
    , In(<'propose', $L, $slot, cmd, $I, capsig, psig>)
    , !Ltk($R, skR)
    , !Pk($L, pkL)
    , !Pk($I, pkI)
    ]
  --[ Eq(verify(psig, <'propose', $slot, cmd>, pkL), true)
    , Eq(verify(capsig, <'cap', cmd>, pkI), true)
    , Voted($R, $slot, cmd)
    , CapCheckedBy($R, $I, cmd)
    ]->
    [ Out(<'vote', $R, $slot, cmd, sign(<'vote', $slot, cmd>, skR)>) ]

// The leader bundles two distinct votes into a QUORUM CERTIFICATE and
// broadcasts it. Note what is NOT here: no authority. Anyone -- including the
// adversary -- can assemble a QC from votes it observed; that is harmless
// precisely because `Apply` checks the certificate rather than the sender.
rule Commit_broadcast:
    [ In(<'vote', $R1, $slot, cmd, s1>)
    , In(<'vote', $R2, $slot, cmd, s2>)
    , !Ltk($L, skL)
    ]
  --[ Neq($R1, $R2)
    , CommitBroadcast($L, $slot, cmd)
    ]->
    [ Out(<'commit', $slot, cmd, <'qc', $R1, s1, $R2, s2>,
           sign(<'commit', $slot, cmd>, skL)>) ]

// A replica applies a committed command -- after verifying THE CERTIFICATE,
// not the sender. The commit's own signature `csig` is bound and then
// deliberately ignored: `Apply` never checks it, and every lemma below still
// holds. That is the machine-checked content of "the leader is not trusted".
rule Apply:
    [ In(<'commit', $slot, cmd, <'qc', $R1, s1, $R2, s2>, csig>)
    , !Pk($R1, pk1)
    , !Pk($R2, pk2)
    ]
  --[ Eq(verify(s1, <'vote', $slot, cmd>, pk1), true)
    , Eq(verify(s2, <'vote', $slot, cmd>, pk2), true)
    , Neq($R1, $R2)
    , Applied($A, $slot, cmd)
    ]->
    [ ]

// =========================================================================
// Lemmas -- each one is a requirement on the R6.1b wire format
// =========================================================================

// Lemma 1 -- QUORUM BACKING. Nothing is applied that a quorum did not vote
// for. This is the wire-level shadow of the premise `DLCD.agreement` reasons
// from, and it is what stops an attacker from installing a command into an
// honest replica's log by fabricating a commit message.
lemma applied_implies_quorum:
  all-traces
  "All A s c #i.
       Applied(A, s, c) @ #i
    ==>
       ( Ex R1 R2 #j #k.
             Voted(R1, s, c) @ #j & Voted(R2, s, c) @ #k
           & not(R1 = R2) & #j < #i & #k < #i )
     | (Ex X #r. Reveal(X) @ #r)"

// Lemma 2 -- CAPABILITY PROVENANCE SURVIVES THE WIRE. If an honest replica
// applied a command, that command's capability really was issued by the
// principal named in it.
//
// This is the lemma that matters most for the overall program: Lean's
// `rust_capability_safety` is CONDITIONED on `DLCD.Authorized` of the decoded
// command. In-process that premise is assumed. This lemma is what will let the
// networked node DISCHARGE it -- the wire structure (capability signature
// checked by every voter, votes checked by the applier) carries authorization
// end-to-end, under an active attacker.
lemma applied_implies_capability:
  all-traces
  "All A s c #i.
       Applied(A, s, c) @ #i
    ==>
       (Ex I #j. CapIssued(I, c) @ #j & #j < #i)
     | (Ex X #r. Reveal(X) @ #r)"

// Lemma 3 -- SLOT AGREEMENT (the crux). Two replicas that apply at the same
// slot apply the SAME command -- even though `Apply` never authenticates the
// sender of the commit, i.e. EVEN IF THE LEADER IS BYZANTINE.
//
// It is true because of exactly two facts, and it is worth naming them since
// the implementation must preserve both: (a) each roster member votes at most
// once per slot (`unique_slot_open` + the linear token), and (b) two quorums
// of 2 out of the 3 roster members must share a member. Quorum intersection --
// `DLCD.quorum_intersect` in Lean -- realized on the wire.
lemma slot_agreement:
  all-traces
  "All A1 A2 s c1 c2 #i #j.
       Applied(A1, s, c1) @ #i & Applied(A2, s, c2) @ #j
    ==>
       c1 = c2
     | (Ex X #r. Reveal(X) @ #r)"

// Lemma 4 -- EXECUTABILITY (anti-vacuity for lemmas 1-3).
//
// The three all-traces lemmas above are implications; if `Applied` could never
// fire they would all hold for the empty set of traces and Tamarin would
// happily report `verified`. That is not hypothetical here: `dlc.spthy`
// carried exactly that defect in `secrecy_ltk` until 2026-07-20 (see its
// comments), and the missing paired exists-trace lemma is why it survived.
// This lemma demands a real run: a capability issued, a proposal, two votes,
// a certificate, an application -- with NO key compromise anywhere.
lemma exec_apply:
  exists-trace
  "Ex A s c #i.
       Applied(A, s, c) @ #i
     & (Ex I #j. CapIssued(I, c) @ #j)
     & not (Ex X #r. Reveal(X) @ #r)"

// Lemma 5 -- the capability check is REACHED, not merely present.
//
// `applied_implies_capability` would also hold if no replica ever checked a
// capability but nothing was ever applied. This pins that the checking path
// itself executes on an honest trace.
lemma exec_cap_checked:
  exists-trace
  "Ex R I c s #i #j.
       CapCheckedBy(R, I, c) @ #i & Voted(R, s, c) @ #j
     & not (Ex X #r. Reveal(X) @ #r)"

end
